# 0011. Job search API: jobs with their match and application status, keyset pages

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-13

One resource, `/api/v1/jobs`, answers "which jobs should I look at": each open job with its
match against your profile and your application status, filtered with Postgres full-text
search and paged with a keyset cursor. There is no separate `/matches` resource.

## Technical details

**Problem.** SPEC Phase 4 asks for a job discovery API, match results, the application
lifecycle and search with filters. Jobs, match results (ADR 0010) and applications (ADR 0008)
are stored, but nothing lets you browse them. The ARCHITECTURE sketch had both `/jobs` and
`/matches`; a match is always about a job, so one resource with the match inside avoids two
ways to list the same thing.

**`GET /api/v1/jobs`**, list items without the description (it can be 20 KB per job):
```
id, title, company_name, location_text, remote_policy, employment_type,
salary {min, max, currency, period} | null, posted_at,
sources [{kind, url, attribution}],         -- Himalayas: attribution "via Himalayas" (terms)
match {score, band, confidence, completeness, hard_constraints, model_version, stale} | null,
application {status}                        -- "discovered" when untouched
```

Query parameters, all optional:

| Param | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `q` | full-text search over title, company and description (Postgres `websearch_to_tsquery`, English) |
| `band` | one or more of strong, good, weak, excluded (repeat the param). **Default: all but excluded**, which failed one of your hard constraints |
| `min_score` | 0-100 |
| `remote` | remote, hybrid, onsite, unknown |
| `source` | greenhouse, lever, ashby, himalayas |
| `status` | application status, including `discovered` |
| `posted_after` | RFC 3339 time |
| `sort` | `score` (default: score, then newest), `posted`, `updated` |
| `cursor`, `limit` | keyset page; limit 1-100, default 25 |
| `profile_id` | default: your only profile (as in ADR 0008) |

- **Which match.** The result of the champion version for the profile's current revision
  and the job's current content. If there is none yet, the newest result of any version or
  older content, with `stale: true` (ADR 0010). If there is none at all, `match` is null:
  the job is pending, and with `sort=score` it comes after scored jobs, never mixed in.
- **Search.** A generated `tsvector` column on `jobs` (title weight A, company B, description
  C) with a GIN index; results matching `q` are ordered by the chosen sort, not by text rank,
  so paging stays stable. Postgres only: no search engine (SPEC §13).
- **Paging.** The cursor is an opaque, URL-safe token of the last item's sort key and id.
  The next page continues after it, so new jobs arriving between pages neither repeat nor
  shift items, unlike offset paging. A cursor from another sort or filter set is rejected
  (422).

**`GET /api/v1/jobs/{id}`**: everything about one job, open or closed:
- the job with `description_text`, all its postings (`sources`, with URLs and attribution)
- `match`: the same choice as above, plus `dimensions`, `skills` (matched, transferable,
  missing) and `explanations` from the stored result
- `application` with its `events` (ADR 0008)

**Performance.** One query per page: a `LATERAL` subquery picks each job's match
(`match_results` is indexed on job, revision and version), a `LEFT JOIN` adds the
application. At the expected 10k-50k jobs this needs no materialized view; a migration adds
the `tsvector` column, its GIN index and an index for the default sort.

**Privacy.** The API serves your profile's results to you on localhost (ADR 0010 guard); the
explanations mention your skills and salary floor, so responses are never logged.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Postgres full-text search (`tsvector`, GIN, `websearch_to_tsquery`), sqlc.
- Alternatives:
  - A separate `/matches` resource: the same jobs listed twice, with two filter sets to keep
    in sync.
  - Offset pagination: simpler, but pages shift as jobs arrive every sync.
  - `ILIKE` search: no index use on long descriptions, no stemming ("pipelines" vs
    "pipeline").
  - Elasticsearch or Meilisearch: another service to run for a few thousand searches a day.
  - Ranking by text relevance: unstable pages and less useful than your match score.
